Flash Sale Engine

A NestJS monorepo that handles a high-traffic online "flash sale". Customers fill a cart and submit it as an order. Stock is checked and reserved in Redis so the API stays fast, and everything slow (saving the order, sending emails) happens in a separate background service.

Stack: NestJS, TypeScript, MySQL (TypeORM), Redis (stock cache + Pub/Sub), BullMQ (job queue), Resend (email), JWT auth.

Architecture
 Customer
    |
    v
+----------------------+                       +---------------------------+
|  flash-sale (API)    |                       |  fulfillment-service      |
|                      |                       |                           |
|  1. read cart (MySQL)|                       |  1. receive event         |
|  2. DECRBY stock     |   order.created       |  2. save order + items    |
|     in Redis         | --- Redis Pub/Sub --> |     (MySQL transaction)   |
|  3. publish event    |                       |  3. add job to BullMQ     |
|  4. clear cart       |                       |  4. worker sends email    |
|  5. return 202       |                       |     (retries on failure)  |
+----------------------+                       |  5. daily cron: emails    |
                                               |     abandoned carts       |
                                               +---------------------------+
App / lib	Path	Responsibility
flash-sale	apps/flash-sale	REST API: auth, products, carts, orders, inventory
fulfillment-service	apps/fulfillment-service	Event subscriber, order persistence, BullMQ worker, cron job
common	libs/common	Shared Redis service and the OrderCreatedEvent contract
Why it is built this way
Redis DECRBY is atomic, so thousands of simultaneous buyers cannot oversell stock, and the stock check never touches MySQL.
Cache-aside stock loading: the first order for a product loads its stock from MySQL into Redis. Nothing is loaded at startup.
Pub/Sub decouples the apps. The API does not wait for the database write or the email.
BullMQ makes emails reliable. Failed sends are retried with exponential backoff (5s, 15s, 45s). Permanent errors (such as an invalid address) are not retried.
The server computes prices and reads the customer from the JWT. The client never sends a total or an email.
Requirements
Node.js 20 or newer
Docker (for MySQL and Redis), or your own MySQL 8 and Redis 7 instances
A free Resend account and API key
Setup
1. Install dependencies
bash
npm install
2. Configure the environment

Copy the example file and fill it in:

bash
cp .env.example .env
Variable	Description
DB_HOST, DB_PORT	MySQL host and port
DB_USERNAME, DB_PASSWORD	MySQL credentials
DB_NAME	Database name (flash_sale_db)
JWT_SECRET	Long random string used to sign tokens
RESEND_API_KEY	Your Resend API key (starts with re_)
ABANDONED_AFTER_MINUTES	Optional. Minutes before a cart counts as abandoned (default 1440, which is 24 hours)

Never commit .env. It is listed in .gitignore.

3. Start MySQL and Redis
bash
docker compose up -d

This creates the database from db/schema.sql and loads sample products from db/seed.sql. It runs once, the first time the MySQL container is created.

If you use your own MySQL, run db/schema.sql yourself. The project uses synchronize: false, so TypeORM never changes your tables.

4. Run the two services

Use two terminals from the project root:

bash
nest start flash-sale --watch
bash
nest start fulfillment-service --watch

The API listens on http://localhost:3000/api/v1 unless you changed the port.

5. Create the admin account

There is exactly one way to become an admin: promote an account in the database. The register endpoint never accepts a role.

Register a normal user with POST /api/v1/auth/register.
Promote it:
sql
UPDATE customers SET role = 'ADMIN' WHERE email = 'admin@example.com';
Log in again. The role is stored inside the token, so an old token keeps its old role until it expires.
API

Base path: /api/v1. Protected routes need the header Authorization: Bearer <accessToken>.

Method	Route	Access	Description
POST	/auth/register	Public	Create a customer account (and its cart)
POST	/auth/login	Public	Returns an accessToken
GET	/products	Public	List products
GET	/products/:id	Public	Get one product
POST	/products	Admin	Create a product (with initial stock)
PATCH	/products/:id	Admin	Update a product
DELETE	/products/:id	Admin	Delete a product
PATCH	/products/:id/stock	Admin	Add stock to a product
GET, POST	/inventory/:productId/stock	Admin	Read or set the Redis stock directly
POST	/carts/items	Customer	Add a product to your cart { "productId": 3, "quantity": 1 }
GET	/carts	Customer	View your cart
POST	/orders	Customer	Submit your cart as an order (no body needed)
Order responses
Status	Meaning
202 Accepted	Order placed. It is saved and emailed in the background
400 Bad Request	Cart is empty, or Out of stock: <product>
401 Unauthorized	Missing or invalid token
403 Forbidden	Admin-only route called by a regular customer
Background jobs

Order receipt. When fulfillment-service receives order.created, it saves the order and its items in one transaction, subtracts the quantities from products.stock, and queues an order-receipt job in notifications-queue. The worker sends an HTML receipt through Resend.

Retry rules. Each job has 4 attempts (1 try + 3 retries) with delays of 5s, 15s and 45s. Server errors (5xx) and rate limits (429) are retried. Other 4xx errors fail immediately.

Abandoned carts. A cron job queries carts that are ACTIVE, not empty, and untouched for 24 hours. For each one it queues an abandoned-cart email and marks the cart ABANDONED, so nobody is emailed twice. Adding a new item sets the cart back to ACTIVE.

By default the cron runs at midnight (CronExpression.EVERY_DAY_AT_MIDNIGHT). For a demo, switch it to EVERY_MINUTE in abandoned-cart.cron.ts and set ABANDONED_AFTER_MINUTES=1.

Demo and tests
Flash sale test: 5 orders accepted, 5 rejected
Set the stock for your test product (edit PRODUCT_ID at the top of scripts/flash-test.js to match):
sql
UPDATE products SET stock = 5 WHERE id = 3;
Clear the Redis key so the stock reloads from MySQL:
bash
docker exec -it redis redis-cli DEL item:3:stock
Run the script. It registers 10 users, fills each cart, and fires all 10 orders at the same moment:
bash
node scripts/flash-test.js

Expected result: five 202 and five 400 Out of stock.

Check the stock: docker exec -it redis redis-cli GET item:3:stock should print 0.

The test users have @example.com addresses, so their receipt emails fail on purpose (see the Resend note below). That does not affect the stock test.

Email receipt test
Register a customer with the same email address as your Resend account.
Log in, add a product to the cart, and call POST /orders.
The fulfillment terminal shows Saved order ..., then completed. The receipt arrives in your inbox. Check the spam folder, because Resend's test sender is often filtered.
Retry test

Temporarily add throw new Error('fake 5xx'); as the first line of sendReceipt in notifications.processor.ts, then place an order. The log shows attempts 1/4 to 4/4 about 5s, 15s and 45s apart. Remove the line afterwards.

Abandoned cart test

Add an item to a cart without ordering, then backdate it:

sql
UPDATE carts SET last_updated_at = NOW() - INTERVAL 2 DAY WHERE id = 1;

Within a minute (with the cron set to every minute) the fulfillment terminal logs Found 1 abandoned cart(s) and the email is sent.

Known limitations
Pub/Sub does not store messages. If fulfillment-service is down or a handler fails when an order is published, the customer has received 202 but no order is saved. A durable queue or the transactional outbox pattern would fix this.
Redis and MySQL can drift. Stock is decremented in Redis immediately and in MySQL later. Lost events leave them out of sync. A reconciliation job would correct this.
A cart can be submitted twice at the same time. Nothing locks the cart during checkout.
The cron marks a cart ABANDONED even if its email later fails.
Resend free tier only delivers to the email address you signed up with. A real deployment needs a verified domain and a from address on it.
Redis should not evict or expire stock keys during a sale. Run it with --appendonly yes and maxmemory-policy noeviction.
Project structure
flash-sale-monorepo/
├── apps/
│   ├── flash-sale/                 # REST API
│   │   └── src/{auth,carts,customers,inventory,orders,products}
│   └── fulfillment-service/        # Subscriber, worker, cron
│       └── src/{notifications,orders}
├── libs/
│   └── common/                     # Redis service, shared event types
├── db/
│   ├── schema.sql                  # Database structure
│   └── seed.sql                    # Sample products
├── scripts/
│   └── flash-test.js               # Concurrency test
├── docker-compose.yml
├── .env.example
└── nest-cli.json